/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/logs/chunks/transcript/00000003.jsonl:48:{"step_index":272,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T09:34:29Z","content":"Here is my complete breakdown of your architecture:\n\n---\n\n### Architecture & Traffic Flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor User as User / Browser\n    participant Web as Centreon Web Backend<br/>(172.30.198.49)\n    participant FW as Firewall / VIP<br/>(109.62.64.18)\n    participant RP as Nginx Reverse Proxy<br/>(172.30.198.33)\n    participant Map as Centreon Map Backend<br/>(172.30.198.50:9443)\n\n    Note over User,Web: 1. Normal Centreon Web usage (Port 443)\n    User->>RP: HTTPS :443 (extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr)\n    RP->>Web: Proxy pass to Centreon Web (:80)\n\n    Note over User,Map: 2. Opening Centreon Map View\n    Web-->>User: Provides Map link / URL targeting 109.62.64.18:9443\n    User->>FW: Request https://map.extranetclient...:9443 (Public IP 109.62.64.18)\n    \n    Note over FW,RP: 3. Firewall DNAT Translation\n    FW->>RP: DNAT 109.62.64.18:9443 -> 172.30.198.33:9443\n\n    Note over RP,Map: 4. Reverse Proxy Routing & Redirection\n    RP->>RP: Matches server_name & port 9443<br/>Redirects / to /centreon-map/\n    RP->>Map: Proxy pass to https://172.30.198.50:9443/centreon-map/\n    Map-->>RP: Returns Map application response / API stream\n    RP-->>FW: Sends response back\n    FW-->>User: Delivers Centreon Map interface to the user\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Breakdown\n\n1. **User & Centreon Web (`172.30.198.49`)**:\n   * The user is logged into the Centreon Web interface (`extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` on port `443`).\n   * When the user clicks on the **Map** module, Centreon Web references the Map server via its configured external address: **`109.62.64.18:9443`** (or `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`).\n\n2. **The Firewall DNAT (`109.62.64.18:9443` $\\rightarrow$ `172.30.198.33:9443`)**:\n   * The request for `109.62.64.18:9443` arrives at the firewall perimeter.\n   * The firewall performs Destination NAT (**DNAT**), translating the public destination IP `109.62.64.18` to the internal IP of the reverse proxy: **`172.30.198.33` on port `9443`**.\n\n3. **The Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33`)**:\n   * The packet lands on your reverse proxy `th2-revproxyp-fe1` on port `9443`.\n   * Nginx terminates the TLS connection, matches the virtual host, handles the root path redirect to `/centreon-map/`, and forwards the request internally.\n\n4. **The Target Centreon Map Server (`172.30.198.50`)**:\n   * Nginx proxies the traffic locally over the private network to the actual Map server at **`https://172.30.198.50:9443/centreon-map/`**.\n   * Centreon Map (Tomcat) serves the view, widgets, and WebSocket data back through Nginx to the user.\n\n---\n\n### Crucial Network Checkpoint for this Flow\n\nIf the connection is initiated by **the user's browser**, it follows standard external inbound DNAT.\n\nHowever, if **Centreon Web (`172.30.198.49`) itself** makes direct server-to-server API calls to Map using the public IP `109.62.64.18:9443`, the Firewall must support **Hairpin NAT (NAT loopback)** so that an internal host (`172.30.198.49`) can reach a public VIP (`109.62.64.18`) that loops back to an internal host (`172.30.198.33`).\n\nThe flow is understood. What would you like to inspect or verify next?","thinking":"**Deconstructing Network Request Flow**\n\nThe user describes a network flow involving a backend, a map server (potentially with a public IP), and a reverse proxy. The request originates at the backend, goes to the map server through the reverse proxy, and is then redirected back through the proxy. Understanding the DNAT aspect is crucial; the reverse proxy appears to be handling this translation. The overall path appears to be backend -> reverse proxy -> map server (DNAT) -> reverse proxy.\n\n\n**Summarizing The Request Flow**\n\nThe user initiates a request from Centreon Web, which is then directed through a firewall's DNAT rule, changing the destination IP to the Reverse Proxy's internal IP. The Reverse Proxy terminates the SSL/TLS connection and then redirects the request, which appears to be hitting the Centreon Map service. This confirms the initial understanding of the data flow.\n\n\n"}
/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/logs/chunks/transcript/00000003.jsonl:66:{"step_index":290,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T09:45:15Z","content":"The network diagnostic test confirms the exact state of the whole chain:\n\n---\n\n### The Verdict\n\n1. **The Application & Proxy Chain**: <span style=\"color:green\">**100% OPERATIONAL**</span>\n   When connecting from backend `172.30.198.49` directly to the Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33:9443`), it connects and returns the Centreon Map application response:\n   ```text\n   HTTP/2 401 Unauthorized\n   Content-Type: application/json;charset=ISO-8859-1\n   {\"code\":401,\"message\":\"Full authentication is required to access this resource\"}\n   ```\n   * The Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33`) properly redirects and proxies to Centreon Map (`172.30.198.50:9443`).\n\n2. **The Public VIP Route (`109.62.64.18:9443`)**: <span style=\"color:red\">**DROPPED BY FIREWALL (Hairpin NAT)**</span>\n   During a synchronized packet capture on `172.30.198.33` while `172.30.198.49` sent requests to `https://109.62.64.18:9443/`:\n   ```text\n   === TCPDUMP ON REVERSE PROXY (172.30.198.33) ===\n   0 packets captured\n   ```\n   **Zero packets reached `172.30.198.33`**. The firewall (`172.30.198.54`) dropped the packet at the perimeter.\n\n---\n\n### Why the Firewall Drops It: Missing \"Hairpin NAT\"\n\nIn enterprise firewalls (FortiGate, Palo Alto, Check Point), an inbound DNAT/VIP rule on the WAN interface **only matches traffic originating from the Internet**.\n\nWhen an internal server (`172.30.198.49`) tries to reach its own public VIP (`109.62.64.18`):\n* The firewall ignores the WAN DNAT rule for internal traffic and silently drops it unless **Hairpin NAT (U-turn NAT)** is explicitly configured.\n\n---\n\n### How to Fix It (2 Options)\n\n#### Option 1: Split-DNS or `/etc/hosts` on Backend (Instant Fix — Recommended)\nOn Centreon Web (`172.30.198.49`), map the hostname directly to the reverse proxy internal IP:\n```bash\n# Add to /etc/hosts on 172.30.198.49\n172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n```\n* **Result**: `172.30.198.49` communicates directly with `172.30.198.33:9443` over the LAN with zero firewall overhead, optimal throughput, and zero external dependency.\n\n#### Option 2: Firewall Hairpin NAT Policy\nOn your firewall (`172.30.198.54`):\n1. Apply the DNAT / VIP rule to the **internal interface** (LAN/DMZ) as well as the WAN.\n2. Enable **SNAT (Source NAT / Masquerade)** on that rule.\n   *(SNAT is mandatory here to prevent asymmetric routing, since `.49` and `.33` are in the same `172.30.198.0/24` subnet).*","thinking":"**Confirming direct service accessibility**\n\nDirectly accessing the reverse proxy from the backend using a `curl` request yielded an immediate `401 Unauthorized` response. This suggests the reverse proxy is operational and accessible from the backend. The entire application chain appears to be functioning correctly based on this test.\n\n\n"}
