ntreon-map/` with **zero SSL certificate warnings**.\n\n---\n\n### Next Action\n\nYou can now click **\"Test connection to server\"** in Centreon Web (`Administration > Extensions > Options`). The test will validate with a green success mark."}
/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:389:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/logs/chunks/transcript_full/00000011.jsonl:38:{"step_index":818,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:51:37Z","content":"As requested, **no connection was made to any server**.\n\nBased on the architecture and the screenshots, here is the complete breakdown of the flow, how the **\"Test connection to server\"** button works, and the exact reasons why it is returning a failed connection.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Understanding the 3 Settings in Centreon MAP\n\nIn your screenshot, there are 3 distinct connection parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Value in Screenshot | Status | Role & Flow |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **MAP Legacy server address** | `172.30.198.50:8080` | <span style=\"color:green\">**SUCCESS**</span> | Legacy HTTP port for the older desktop app. Central (`.49`) connects directly over LAN to `.50:8080`. |\n| **MAP server address as seen by Central** | `https://th2-centprop-map1.prod.intranet...:9443` | <span style=\"color:green\">**SUCCESS**</span> | **Backend-to-Backend**. Central (`.49`) talks directly to Map (`.50:9443`) over internal network/DNS. |\n| **MAP Engine server address** | `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-tele...` | <span style=\"color:red\">**FAILED**</span> | **External Client URL**. The public URL given to user web browsers to display the Map interface. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Complete Flow of the Chain\n\nDepending on whether Centreon Web tests this button from the **Backend (PHP)** or from the **Client Browser (JavaScript)**, the traffic takes one of two paths:\n\n#### Path A: The Test is Run by the Central Backend (`th2-centprop-be1`)\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Central as Central Backend (172.30.198.49)\n    participant RevProxy as Reverse Proxy (172.30.198.33:9443)\n    participant Map as Map Tomcat (172.30.198.50:9443)\n\n    Central->>RevProxy: 1. Resolves via /etc/hosts -> 172.30.198.33:9443\n    Central->>RevProxy: 2. TLS Handshake (Valid Let's Encrypt Cert)\n    Central->>RevProxy: 3. HTTP GET to URL configured in the field\n    alt If URL is root (https://map...:9443/)\n        RevProxy-->>Central: 4. Returns HTTP 301 Redirect to /centreon-map/\n        Note over Central: If PHP cURL does NOT follow redirects,<br/>it treats 301 as a FAILURE!\n    else If URL is /centreon-map/\n        RevProxy->>Map: 4. proxy_pass https://172.30.198.50:9443\n        Map-->>RevProxy: 5. Map responds (HTTP 400 / 401)\n        RevProxy-->>Central: 6. Proxied response returned to PHP\n    end\n```\n\n#### Path B: The Test is Run by Your Browser (Client-Side AJAX)\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Browser as Your Browser (User Workstation)\n    participant PubDNS as Public DNS (109.62.64.18)\n    participant Firewall as Firewall Gateway (172.30.198.54)\n    participant RevProxy as Reverse Proxy (172.30.198.33:9443)\n\n    Browser->>PubDNS: 1. Resolves map.extranetclient... -> 109.62.64.18\n    Browser->>Firewall: 2. TCP SYN to 109.62.64.18:9443\n    alt Inside Corporate Network (Hairpin NAT Issue)\n        Note over Firewall: If your workstation is inside corporate LAN/VLAN,<br/>the firewall drops traffic to its own public VIP!\n        Firewall--xBrowser: 3. Connection Timeout / Refused\n    else Outside on Internet (Direct WAN)\n        Firewall->>RevProxy: 3. WAN DNAT forwards to 172.30.198.33:9443\n        RevProxy-->>Browser: 4. TLS Handshake OK\n        Note over Browser: CORS Check: Browser on port 443 calls port 9443.<br/>If CORS headers are missing, browser blocks it!\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. The 4 Root Causes Why the Test Button Still Fails\n\n#### Cause 1: Missing Port `:9443` in the Input Box\n* In Screenshot 1, the field contains: `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-tele...`\n* If the string in the field is **`https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`** without explicitly specifying **`:9443`**, the request will connect to port **`443`**.\n* Port `443` on the reverse proxy is **Centreon Web**, not Centreon Map! When it tries to reach Map APIs on port 443, it receives a 404 or redirect, causing the test to fail.\n* **Expected format**: `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`\n\n#### Cause 2: PHP cURL Does Not Follow `301 Redirect`\n* When testing `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`, Nginx currently returns **`301 Moved Permanently`** redirecting to `/centreon-map/`.\n* In PHP, standard `curl_exec()` **does not follow redirects** unless `CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION = true` is set. \n* A `301` status is not a `200` status, so Centreon Web flags it with a red **X**.\n* **Test**: Try entering the full path in the box:  \n  `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/centreon-map/`\n\n#### Cause 3: Hairpin NAT from Your Workstation\n* If the button initiates the test from your browser via JavaScript:\n  * Your PC resolves `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` to the public IP **`109.62.64.18`**.\n  * As we found earlier with Central, the firewall gateway does **not** have Hairpin NAT enabled.\n  * If your workstation is on the internal network, trying to connect to its own public IP `109.62.64.18:9443` gets dropped at the firewall.\n  * *(An external user connecting from the outside internet does not have this problem because their traffic comes through WAN).*\n\n#### Cause 4: Browser CORS Block (Cross-Origin)\n* The user is browsing Centreon Web on **port 443**: `https://extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/`.\n* The test button requests **port 9443**: `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/`.\n* Different ports and subdomains are considered a **different Origin** by modern web browsers. If the reverse proxy does not return CORS headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`), the browser silently blocks the request and reports `Connection test failed`.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist to Resolve\n\n1. **Verify the exact URL in the field**:\n   Must include the port:\n   `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`  \n   *(or `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/centreon-map`)*\n2. **Press F12 in your browser (Developer Tools > Console & Network tab)**:\n   Click the \"Test connection to server\" button and check:\n   * If a red error appears in the browser **Console** $\\rightarrow$ it is a **Browser CORS** or **Network Timeout** issue (Client-side).\n   * If a network request to `/centreon/...` returns 200 with `{\"status\": false}` $\\rightarrow$ it is a **PHP cURL 301 redirect** issue (Server-side).","thinking":"**Investigating MAP Engine connection issues**\n\nConnection test failure observed for the specified MAP Engine server address, `https://109.62.64.18:9443`. Suspect the address from a different screenshot might be the correct one, though the port `:9443` is possibly missing. Need to confirm if the address in Screenshot 1, which ends with an ellipsis, also includes the port.\n\n\n**Analyzing Connection Test Execution**\n\nThe connection test's execution point is crucial; is it browser-side JavaScript or server-side PHP? If the PHP backend performs the test, it likely utilizes cURL on the provided URL. The server would then test the engine's availability on various endpoints. The earlier `HTTP/2 400` response from a manual cURL test raises a key question.\n\nThe HTTP 400 error strongly indicates a problem with the requested endpoint or input. It is vital to determine the root cause of this error to establish a successful connection to the MAP engine.\n\n\n**Tracing Connection Test Flow**\n\nAnalyze the exact test endpoint used by Centreon Web's Map extension to confirm it matches expectations. Re-examine the nginx configuration, specifically how headers are passed and the `proxy_pass` directive, and how those settings might affect the client request. Confirm the expected endpoint the Tomcat server for Centreon Map is listening on.\n\n**Investigating MAP Engine connection issues**\n\nConnection test failure observed for the specified MAP Engine server address, `https://109.62.64.18:9443`. Suspect the address from a different screenshot might be the correct one, though the port `:9443` is possibly missing. Need to confirm if the address in Screenshot 1, which ends with an ellipsis, also includes the port.\n\n\n**Analyzing Connection Test Execution**\n\nThe connection test's execution point is crucial; is it browser-side JavaScript or server-side PHP? If the PHP backend performs the test, it likely utilizes cURL on the provided URL. The server would then test the engine's availability on various endpoints. The earlier `HTTP/2 400` response from a manual cURL test raises a key question.\n\nThe HTTP 400 error strongly indicates a problem with the requested endpoint or input. It is vital to determine the root cause of this error to establish a successful connection to the MAP engine.\n\n**Examining Request Header Handling**\n\nThe `nginx` configuration sets several headers, including `Host` and `X-Forwarded-*`. It is imperative to determine how the final `Host` header reaching the backend Tomcat server is constructed, given the interplay of `$http_host` and `$host`. These values potentially overwrite each other. The `401 Unauthorized` response observed earlier and the later `400` error could stem from incorrect headers or an invalid endpoint.\n\n\n**Evaluating Test Request Origin**\n\nDetermine if the failing test originates from Centreon Web's backend PHP or a browser-side JavaScript call. If PHP, the DNS resolution and reverse proxy are critical paths. Check `/etc/hosts` for direct `map.extranetclient...` resolution, because this might bypass the configured DNS for the test.\n\n**Tracing Server-Side Request Path**\n\nIf server-side, confirm how the reverse proxy (`nginx`) forwards the request to the Map server, including the `proxy_pass` configuration. The endpoint `/centreon-map/` or `/centreon-map/api/...` must also be examined, as it could result in the `400` error if invalid. Examine if the proxy forwards headers that might cause a `400`.\n\n**Investigating Client-Side Execution Path**\n\nIf the test runs client-side, the browser directly connects to `map.extranetclient...` and the network route and local DNS settings become relevant. Analyze what test request is sent to `map.extranetclient...`, including headers, and determine the exact endpoint it attempts to reach. I will verify if the `Host` header sent by the client is the same as the configured server-side `Host` header.\n\n\n**Analyzing PHP Server-Side Checks**\n\nPHP's cURL behavior warrants inspection; it defaults to *not* following redirects, leading to failure if a `301` redirect to `/centreon-map/` occurs. This implies a possible issue if the provided URL lacks the explicit port `:9443` or has a trailing slash.\n\n**Evaluating Client-Side Browser Path**\n\nClient-side tests involve the browser, and the DNS resolution translates `map.extranetclient...` to a public IP. Network routing and firewall rules must permit traffic on port `9443` to the target address, which may require hairpin NAT inside the corporate network.\n\n**Investigating Client-Side CORS**\n\nClient-side testing must consider CORS. The browser is on a different origin, and the test request requires CORS configuration to be successful.\n\n\n"}
