he DNS record to `109.62.62.18` (or change the DNAT rule to `109.62.64.18`).\n> * If the DNAT is indeed on **`109.62.64.18`**, make sure the rule and port `9443` security policy are committed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Testing from External Network\n\n* When testing from **inside the corporate LAN/datacenter**, connecting to the public IP may time out due to a lack of **NAT Hairpinning / Loopback NAT** on the firewall.\n* **Test from an external connection** (e.g. mobile 4G/5G or home connection):\n  ```bash\n  curl -k -i -L https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/\n  ```\n  *(Accept the certificate warning in your browser for now, as the certificate is currently issued for `extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`).*","thinking":"**Observing redirection and authentication**\n\nThe initial request resulted in a redirection to a secure HTTPS endpoint. Subsequently, an authentication error (401) was received from the server. This indicates that the reverse proxy is successfully redirecting but the subsequent request requires authentication.\n\n\n**Investigating DNS and Firewall Mismatch**\n\nThe DNS record resolves to `109.62.64.18`, while the intended DNAT configuration uses `109.62.62.18`.  This discrepancy suggests the traffic isn't reaching the intended destination if the firewall configuration is indeed on `.62`. Alternatively, if the firewall is configured on `.64`, the issue could be with firewall security rules, or internal network hairpinning.\n\n\n"}
