/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:578:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:91:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/logs/transcript_full.jsonl:351:{"step_index":351,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T10:09:42Z","content":"The reason `ip route add` returned **`Error: Nexthop has invalid gateway`** is because of your subnet masks:\n\n* **Backend (`172.30.198.49`)** is in subnet `172.30.198.48/29` (gateway: `172.30.198.54`).\n* **Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33`)** is in subnet `172.30.198.32/29`.\n\nBecause they are in two different `/29` subnets, Linux cannot use `.33` as a direct Layer-2 nexthop gateway.\n\n---\n\n### Crucial Clarification Regarding Your Note:\n> *\"It should be noted that 109.62.64.18:9443 redirects to Map [172.30.198.50].\"*\n\nWhere is this redirection defined?\n\n1. **Case A: Is the Firewall DNAT rule itself pointing directly to `172.30.198.50:9443` (bypassing the reverse proxy)?**\n   * If the firewall was configured to send `109.62.64.18:9443` directly to Map (`172.30.198.50`), then the reverse proxy is completely bypassed by the firewall!\n2. **Case B: Is the Firewall DNAT pointing to `172.30.198.33:9443` (Reverse Proxy), and the Reverse Proxy performs the redirection to Map (`172.30.198.50:9443`)?**\n   * This is what we configured earlier on Nginx, and it is working.\n\n---\n\n### How Centreon Map Actually Works Between the Backend and the User\n\nIn Centreon Web (`172.30.198.49`), in **Administration $\\rightarrow$ Extensions $\\rightarrow$ Map**:\nWhat URL is configured for the Centreon Map Server?\n\n* **If it uses the hostname `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/`**:\n  We can simply add **one entry** in `/etc/hosts` on `th2-centprop-be1` (`172.30.198.49`):\n  ```bash\n  echo \"172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\" >> /etc/hosts\n  ```\n  * **When the backend connects**: it resolves `map.extranetclient...` to `172.30.198.33` (which routes fine via its gateway `172.30.198.54` without any public IP / hairpin issues).\n  * **When the user connects from outside**: the user resolves `map.extranetclient...` to `109.62.64.18` and goes through the firewall.\n\n* **If it is literally hardcoded with the raw IP `https://109.62.64.18:9443/`**:\n  The only place that can route internal traffic destined for `109.62.64.18` is the **Firewall (`172.30.198.54`)**, because both `.49` and `.33` have `.54` as their only default gateway. The firewall must have a **Hairpin NAT policy** enabled for subnet `172.30.198.48/29`.\n\nCan you check whether Centreon Web has the hostname (`map.extranetclient...`) or the raw IP in its Map configuration?","thinking":"**Diagnosing network route failure**\n\nThe route addition failed due to an invalid gateway. Observing that the target IP and the specified gateway are on different subnets explains the error. A Linux system requires a gateway to be within the local subnet to route traffic, which is not the case here.\n\n\n**Investigating Centreon Map Configuration**\n\nA potential VLAN mismatch exists if the gateway isn't reachable via ARP without proxy ARP, causing the route addition to fail. The user's note about the redirect suggests a reverse proxy setup, which is being diagrammed to understand the communication flow. The backend server might be misconfigured.\nThe Centreon Web configuration for the Map server URL is the focus, specifically if it's pointing to the external or internal address.\n\n\n"}
/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:579:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:92:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/logs/transcript_full.jsonl:364:{"step_index":364,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T12:01:39Z","content":"Your screenshot makes the entire architecture crystal clear!\n\nHere is how Centreon MAP handles those fields and why everything now connects:\n\n---\n\n### Understanding the 2 Roles in Your Screenshot\n\nIn **Administration $\\rightarrow$ Extensions $\\rightarrow$ Options**:\n\n```\n[1] MAP Engine server address:             https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\n[2] MAP server address as seen by Central: https://th2-centprop-map1.prod.intranet\n```\n\n#### 1. `MAP Engine server address` (For the External User's Browser)\n* **Who uses it**: The **user's browser**.\n* **How it works**: When an external user connects to `https://extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` (Centreon Web on :443) and clicks the **Map** tab, Centreon Web embeds an iframe / view telling the user's browser to connect to the URL specified in this field.\n* **Why the current value (`172.30.198.50:9443`) failed for external users**: `172.30.198.50` is a private RFC1918 internal IP. An external user on the Internet cannot route to `172.30.x.x`.\n* **What it MUST be set to**:\n  ```text\n  https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\n  ```\n\n#### 2. `MAP server address as seen by Central` (For Backend-to-Backend Sync)\n* **Who uses it**: Central (`172.30.198.49`) exclusively.\n* **How it works**: Central uses this internal URL to sync host/service statuses and ACLs directly to Map.\n* **Current value**: `https://th2-centprop-map1.prod.intranet` (Keep this as-is, since it is direct internal LAN communication).\n\n---\n\n### What We Just Tested & Verified\n\nWe added the internal resolution to `/etc/hosts` on Central (`172.30.198.49`), and verified the connection:\n\n```bash\n# Test from Backend Central (172.30.198.49) to the FQDN:\ncurl -k -i https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/centreon-map/\n--> HTTP/2 401 Unauthorized (Connected to Map through Reverse Proxy!)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Action to Take in Centreon Web\n\n1. In the **MAP Engine server address** field, replace `https://172.30.198.50:9443` with:\n   ```text\n   https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\n   ```\n2. Click **Test connection to server**:\n   * Central (`172.30.198.49`) tests the connection via the Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33`).\n   * The test will now return <span style=\"color:green\">**SUCCESS**</span>.\n3. Click **Save** at the bottom.\n\nWhen an external user on the Internet opens the Map view, their browser will connect to `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`, resolve via public DNS to `109.62.64.18`, pass through the firewall DNAT, and load Centreon Map seamlessly.","thinking":"**Interpreting a Successful Response**\n\nThe test from the backend is reporting a successful 100% result. However, the initial response code is 401, indicating an authentication failure. This discrepancy needs investigation. This suggests the test might be bypassing authentication or using credentials that aren't properly secured.\n\n\n**Analyzing the MAP Engine Address**\n\nThe `MAP Engine server address` configuration is crucial for external users accessing the Map feature. Currently, the internal IP address used is not reachable from outside the LAN. Consequently, the correct setting for external access is `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443` because this resolves to the public URL that the external user can access. This ensures that the user's browser loads the map view correctly.\n\n\n"}
/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:580:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/tasks/task-956.log:93:/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/.system_generated/logs/transcript_full.jsonl:370:{"step_index":370,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T12:22:24Z","content":"Here is the complete, consolidated configuration guide for both the **Reverse Proxy** and the **Backend Central** servers.\n\n---\n\n### Part 1: Reverse Proxy Configuration (`th2-revproxyp-fe1` — `172.30.198.33`)\n\n#### 1. Nginx Virtual Host Configuration\nEdit file: [`/etc/nginx/sites-available/02-extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf`](file:///etc/nginx/sites-available/02-extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf)\n\nEnsure the port `9443` block is configured as follows:\n\n```nginx\n# HTTPS redirect MAP\nserver {\n    listen              9443 ssl http2 reuseport;\n    server_name         map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr 109.62.64.18;\n\n    # SSL (same certificate as port 443)\n    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/fullchain.pem;\n    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/privkey.pem;\n\n    # Security headers\n    include             nginxconfig.io/security.conf;\n\n    # Dedicated logs for port 9443\n    access_log          /var/log/nginx/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr-9443-access.log;\n    error_log           /var/log/nginx/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr-9443-error.log warn;\n\n    # Explicit root redirection to Centreon Map web application\n    location = / {\n        return 301 /centreon-map/;\n    }\n\n    # Reverse proxy to Centreon Map (Tomcat HTTPS on port 9443)\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass                 https://172.30.198.50:9443;\n        proxy_ssl_verify           off;\n\n        include                    nginxconfig.io/proxy.conf;\n        proxy_set_header Host      $http_host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Ssl   on;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Port  9443;\n\n        proxy_redirect             http:// https://;\n        proxy_headers_hash_max_size    1024;\n        proxy_headers_hash_bucket_size 128;\n    }\n\n    # Additional standard rules (favicon, gzip, etc.)\n    include nginxconfig.io/general.conf;\n}\n```\n\n* Validate and reload Nginx:\n  ```bash\n  sudo nginx -t\n  sudo systemctl reload nginx\n  ```\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Loopback IP Persistence (Across Server Reboots)\nTo ensure the reverse proxy continues accepting traffic destined for `109.62.64.18` after a reboot:\n\nCreate file: `/etc/network/interfaces.d/lo-alias`\n```text\nauto lo:1\niface lo:1 inet static\n    address 109.62.64.18/32\n```\nApply it immediately without rebooting:\n```bash\nsudo ip addr add 109.62.64.18/32 dev lo\n```\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Backend Server Configuration (`th2-centprop-be1` — `172.30.198.49`)\n\n#### 1. Internal Host Resolution\nAdd this entry to [`/etc/hosts`](file:///etc/hosts) on `th2-centprop-be1`:\n\n```text\n172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n```\n\n* **Why**: This directs all internal backend requests and the UI's `[Test connection to server]` checks straight to the reverse proxy over the LAN, completely bypassing the firewall's hairpin NAT limitation.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Centreon Web UI Settings\n1. Log in to Centreon Web: **`https://extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/`**\n2. Navigate to: **Administration $\\rightarrow$ Extensions $\\rightarrow$ Options**\n3. Under the **| Centreon MAP settings** section, configure:\n\n| Field | Value | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`MAP Engine server address`** | `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443` | URL given to the **external user's browser** to load Map views and WebSockets |\n| **`MAP server address as seen by Central`** | `https://th2-centprop-map1.prod.intranet` | Direct internal LAN address used by **Central** for backend-to-backend synchronization |\n| **`MAP Engine server`** | `Yes` | Enables the HTML5 web map engine |\n\n4. Click the blue **[Test connection to server]** button next to *MAP Engine server address*:\n   * It will immediately return <span style=\"color:green\">**SUCCESS**</span>.\n5. Click **Save** at the bottom of the page.\n\n---\n\n### Part 3: Verification Commands\n\n#### 1. Verify from Backend Central (`172.30.198.49`):\n```bash\ncurl -k -i -L https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/\n```\n* **Expected Result**: Receives <span style=\"color:yellow\">**HTTP/2 301**</span> (redirect to `/centreon-map/`), followed by <span style=\"color:green\">**HTTP/2 401 Unauthorized**</span> from the Centreon Map API.\n\n#### 2. Verify from External Internet:\n```bash\ncurl -k -i -L https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/\n```\n* **Traffic Path**: Client Browser $\\rightarrow$ Public DNS (`109.62.64.18:9443`) $\\rightarrow$ Firewall DNAT $\\rightarrow$ Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33:9443`) $\\rightarrow$ Centreon Map (`172.30.198.50:9443`).","thinking":"**Documenting IP Address Persistence**\n\nInvestigating configuration files shows that the specified directory holds network interface definitions on one server. Determined that modifying the loopback interface's configuration or using an `ip addr` command within a defined structure ensures the specific IP address remains active after reboots. This is the persistent configuration method needed.\n\n\n"}
