telecom.fr:9443/`**:\n  We can simply add **one entry** in `/etc/hosts` on `th2-centprop-be1` (`172.30.198.49`):\n  ```bash\n  echo \"172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\" >> /etc/hosts\n  ```\n  * **When the backend connects**: it resolves `map.extranetclient...` to `172.30.198.33` (which routes fine via its gateway `172.30.198.54` without any public IP / hairpin issues).\n  * **When the user connects from outside**: the user resolves `map.extranetclient...` to `109.62.64.18` and goes through the firewall.\n\n* **If it is literally hardcoded with the raw IP `https://109.62.64.18:9443/`**:\n  The only place that can route internal traffic destined for `109.62.64.18` is the **Firewall (`172.30.198.54`)**, because both `.49` and `.33` have `.54` as their only default gateway. The firewall must have a **Hairpin NAT policy** enabled for subnet `172.30.198.48/29`.\n\nCan you check whether Centreon Web has the hostname (`map.extranetclient...`) or the raw IP in its Map configuration?","thinking":"**Diagnosing network route failure**\n\nThe route addition failed due to an invalid gateway. Observing that the target IP and the specified gateway are on different subnets explains the error. A Linux system requires a gateway to be within the local subnet to route traffic, which is not the case here.\n\n\n**Investigating Centreon Map Configuration**\n\nA potential VLAN mismatch exists if the gateway isn't reachable via ARP without proxy ARP, causing the route addition to fail. The user's note about the redirect suggests a reverse proxy setup, which is being diagrammed to understand the communication flow. The backend server might be misconfigured.\nThe Centreon Web configuration for the Map server URL is the focus, specifically if it's pointing to the external or internal address.\n\n\n"}
